# 6. Define canonical severity policy

Create an explicit policy assigning every stable reason code to a canonical severity.

## Hard failures

Hard failures include at minimum:

* wrong equipment identity,
* gym-template inheritance in another primary mode,
* illegal equipment,
* unconfirmed equipment, support or anchor use,
* incorrect band type,
* anchor-height mismatch,
* false vertical or horizontal pulling satisfaction,
* preparation-only movement satisfying a primary strength role,
* missing required primary role where the capability exists,
* day-identity mismatch,
* excessive complexity beyond the contract,
* invalid progression or regression reference,
* deterministic mismatch,
* unreachable release-critical coaching,
* missing complete written coaching for a reachable exercise,
* contradictory exercise setup,
* and a corrupted or unresolvable generated exercise ID.

## Capability limitations

Examples:

* true vertical pulling unavailable with dumbbells alone;
* true loaded pulling unavailable in floor-and-wall bodyweight mode;
* high-anchor pulling unavailable when no secure high anchor is confirmed;
* loop-only bands cannot supply long-band pulling.

These pass only when the program:

* identifies the limitation honestly,
* does not falsely claim the role,
* and remains complete within the physical capability available.

## Warnings

Warnings may include:

* an uncommon but valid exercise family,
* unusually high but still permitted setup complexity,
* a valid terminal progression,
* or another non-blocking review signal.

Warnings must not conceal structural failures.

## Deferred content

Planned exercise videos belong here.

Missing planned videos do not fail Phase 7 when complete written coaching exists.

Document the full code-to-severity map.

